@anon.879c592214345ca999bafaa324406990.831 = private unnamed_addr constant [981 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of comparators other than `<` and `>=` for\0A`sys.version_info` checks. All other comparators, such\0Aas `>`, `<=`, and `==`, are banned.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AComparing `sys.version_info` with `==` or `<=` has unexpected behavior\0Aand can lead to bugs.\0A\0AFor example, `sys.version_info > (3, 8, 1)` will resolve to `True` if your\0APython version is 3.8.1; meanwhile, `sys.version_info <= (3, 8)` will _not_\0Aresolve to `True` if your Python version is 3.8.10:\0A\0A```python\0A>>> import sys\0A>>> print(sys.version_info)\0Asys.version_info(major=3, minor=8, micro=10, releaselevel='final', serial=0)\0A>>> print(sys.version_info > (3, 8))\0ATrue\0A>>> print(sys.version_info == (3, 8))\0AFalse\0A>>> print(sys.version_info <= (3, 8))\0AFalse\0A>>> print(sys.version_info in (3, 8))\0AFalse\0A```\0A\0A## Example\0A```py\0Aimport sys\0A\0Aif sys.version_info > (3, 8): ...\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```py\0Aimport sys\0A\0Aif sys.version_info >= (3, 9): ...\0A```\0A\0A[preview]: https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/preview/\0A", align 1
@anon.879c592214345ca999bafaa324406990.832 = private unnamed_addr constant [50 x i8] c"Use `<` or `>=` for `sys.version_info` comparisons", align 1
